      "costUsd": 0.007683499999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended with a bouncing ball. It's dropped from one hundred and twenty-eight centimetres, and each bounce reaches half the height it fell from: sixty-four, thirty-two, sixteen, and so on. After eight bounces, the heights add to one hundred and twenty-seven and a half. Each new bounce fills exactly half of the gap that's left below one hundred and twenty-eight. So the total keeps growing, but it can never pass one hundred and twenty-eight. Can infinitely many bounces really add to a finite number? And when does that happen?"
    },
