      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 124,
      "outputTokens": 947,
      "costUsd": 0.008584999999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Start with the terms. Here r is a half, so each bounce is half the one before. Halving again and again makes a number as small as you like. After ten halvings, a half to the ten is less than one thousandth. So the terms get closer and closer to zero. The same is true for any r between minus one and one. Multiplying by a number smaller than one in size shrinks the size by the same fraction every time. With r equal to minus a half, the terms flip sign, but their size still halves, so they close in on zero from both sides."
    },
