      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 118,
      "outputTokens": 859,
      "costUsd": 0.00779,
      "pronunciationText": "What goes wrong otherwise? A magic ball that bounced twice as high each time would have r equal to two, and its totals would grow without bound. If r is one, every term is a, so the total grows by a each time. If r is minus one, the terms are a, minus a, a, minus a, and the totals bounce between a and zero forever. They never settle on one number. Whenever the size of r is one or more, r to the n doesn't shrink, so the gap never closes. There's no limiting sum."
    },
